{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>Oil for the lamp.</b> Pure olive oil, beaten, for the lamp, to cause the lamp to burn continually. <b>Spices for the anointing oil and for the incense of spices.</b> By way of its plain meaning, they shall bring the spices for the incense, because the בשמים are the foodstuffs called מסעדים, which are seasoned with fine fragrance, whereas the סמים are the medicinal spices not meant for eating, such as frankincense or galbanum and similar things. And the reason Scripture mentioned gold here at the head of the offering is to hint that the sin of the calf, which was of gold, had already been forgiven them; therefore Scripture mentioned it at the head of all the rest of the offering. And so the Rabbis of blessed memory expounded the fact that Scripture mentions (Leviticus 22:27) שור או כשב או עז כי יולד — that it mentioned שור at the beginning of the passage — as hinting that that sin had already been forgiven them. And for this reason Moses was commanded here concerning the offering for the Tabernacle, and this command was on the morrow of the Day of Atonement, even though that sin preceded the making of the Tabernacle, for the sin was in Tammuz and the making of the Tabernacle was after the Day of Atonement, shortly thereafter; nevertheless the Torah, all whose ways are ways of pleasantness and all whose paths are peace, wished to set forth the matter of the making of the Tabernacle — which constitutes atonement — before it would mention that sin, for such is the measure of the Holy One, blessed be He: that He prepares the remedy before the blow; in the manner of what the Rabbis of blessed memory expounded: the Holy One, blessed be He, creates the remedy for Israel first and afterward smites them, as it is said (Hosea 7:1): כרפאי לישראל ונגלה עון אפרים." "<b>And the hint thereof</b> is to intimate that they had already been forgiven completely; therefore it mentioned the word זהב at the beginning of the offering, to teach you that with the very thing through which they sinned they were reconciled. And so it says (ibid.): ברעתם ישמחו מלך." "<b>By way of the Midrash:</b> זהב וכסף ונחשת — gold corresponds to the kingdom of Babylon, for it is written of Nebuchadnezzar, אנת הוא רישא דדהבא. Silver corresponds to the kingdom of Media, as it is said (Esther 3:9): ועשרת אלפים ככר כסף אשקול. Bronze corresponds to the kingdom of Greece, which is inferior to them all. אבני שהם ואבני מלואים corresponds to the kingdom of Edom, as it is said (Genesis 25:25): ויצא הראשון אדמוני. What is written after it? שמן למאור — this is King Messiah, as it is said (Psalms 132:17–18): שם אצמיח קרן לדוד ערכתי נר למשיחי. בשמים לשמן המשחה — that he is destined to anoint a High Priest. ולקטרת הסמים — in the manner of what is written (Ezekiel 20:41): בריח ניחוח ארצה אתכם. אבני שהם — these are kadkod stones, as it is said (Isaiah 54:12): ושמתי כדכד שמשותיך. ואבני מלואים — these are eben-ekdach stones, as it is said (ibid.): ושעריך לאבני אקדח. ועשו לי מקדש — this is the Holy Temple that shall be rebuilt speedily in our days. ושכנתי בתוכם — לעולם ולעולמי עולמים."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {1 "The lemma וכדי appears to introduce the purpose of the hint; rendered as 'and the hint thereof.' The prooftext ברעתם ישמחו מלך is from Hosea 7:3, not Hosea 7:1 as in the preceding comment; the referent of '(שם)' is ambiguous.", 2 "Lemma reads 'ובמדרש' rendered as the fixed method-header 'By way of the Midrash.' The Aramaic phrase אנת הוא רישא דדהבא (Daniel 2:38) and the closing phrase לעולם ולעולמי עולמים are left in transliteration/Hebrew as they appear without translation."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:04:07.183675861Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}